#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203777 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "1c25c6d9-21b9-59a5-a563-25cb89788286" :question/math-academy-id "e-16706" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1] \\times [0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2y-2x^2+3xy^2+3xy+1.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 1 \\big]^T.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}+3xy+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}+3xy+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4y-4 & 4x+6y+3 \\\\ 4x+6y+3 & 6x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/assets/9a89c30b5dcacbee11ea07f93458a61dd674cc8105af6f0cf5d57898b29b9787.png)\n\nStarting with\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/assets/41167d8f07938db9bfbeadb4e672e602d6dfd9c5d0b293a55952b5e8ec7a7c13.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}4y-4 & 4x+6y+3 \\\\ 4x+6y+3 & 6x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4(1)-4 & 4(1)+6(1)+3 \\\\ 4(1)+6(1)+3 & 6(1)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4(1)(1)-4(1)+3(1)^{2}+3(1) \\\\ 2(1)^{2}+6(1)(1)+3(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 13 \\\\ 13 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{0\\cdot 6-13\\cdot 13}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{169}[\\begin{aligned}-107 \\\\ -78\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.367 \\\\ 0.538\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203778 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "a5e04738-9b21-56d1-b483-703b82e3379a" :question/math-academy-id "e-16707" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2] \\times [1,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^3+y^3-3x-6y+9.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 2 \\big]^T$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $\\mathbf x_1 = \\big[1, \\: 1.5 \\big]^T.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3x-6y+9) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3x-6y+9)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/assets/ff3b9d25f1fe5ae3a7f784980aa6bce1340ea9186537f097222fbea82a596257.png)\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6(1.5)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}-3 \\\\ 3(1.5)^{2}-6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 9-0\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{54}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4.5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.417\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203779 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "e5c61c4c-52a7-52cb-85fe-bc9c3d5e49ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-16708" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times[0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = xy^2+3x^2-xy-x.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix equation that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $\\big[ 1, \\: 1 \\big]^T,$ and compute the components of the first iteration" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nH_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+6x-y-1 \\\\ 2xy-x\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+6x-y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+6x-y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2y-1 \\\\ 2y-1 & 2x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/assets/ddb94c6d067c419b06cdbec87426e70db6ee07acca7d42c0900ae386f6fe37ae.png)\n\nStarting with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/assets/8bdb55b26b462507d9810675499558be66e7b970d2f9fdb78e708807e617ce8d.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2y-1 \\\\ 2y-1 & 2x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+6x-y-1 \\\\ 2xy-x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & 2(1)-1 \\\\ 2(1)-1 & 2(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}+6(1)-(1)-1 \\\\ 2(1)(1)-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/assets/daee26a8f82011d916bf04d7e374a2b8f471602877fe76335f7f1b242ca86b00.png) Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.182 \\\\ 0.909\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cc2e01d-6f80-51b5-b8a9-8049d9e119c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cc2e01d-6f80-51b5-b8a9-8049d9e119c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380974} :answer-field/id #uuid "25e3de80-bb2f-5559-b5a3-362090da0ad2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d38c1ba4-f0b4-54be-934d-689905d21c14" :question/math-academy-id "q-256562" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y-2x-{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}=[$ | $1.25$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-2x-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-2x-{y}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy-2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2y\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x \\\\ 2x & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x \\\\ 2x & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2xy-2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x \\\\ 2x & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2xy-2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(1) & 2(1) \\\\ 2(1) & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2(1)(1)-2 \\\\ (1)^{2}-2(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.75\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf61e214-721b-5633-8cb4-c9d8eadeec1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf61e214-721b-5633-8cb4-c9d8eadeec1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380977} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7053281-3b21-5fb0-8a65-bc903498a6ac" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202989} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19e97a3e-93c8-587c-9545-b6232c6581c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19e97a3e-93c8-587c-9545-b6232c6581c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380975} :answer-field/id #uuid "f08fef05-6bed-5a9c-9740-d6af450c785b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202990} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7aff0d-dc94-543f-a75d-63b98926628c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c7aff0d-dc94-543f-a75d-63b98926628c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380978} :answer-field/id #uuid "32c0b86b-9d32-5fda-9782-bba40b885de4" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202991} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "400c36c5-f750-50af-8f98-0fbc845889fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "400c36c5-f750-50af-8f98-0fbc845889fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380976} :answer-field/id #uuid "4710a21a-0b63-5b4e-90ad-e1b6780cbe88" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de703970-b156-5cd3-a172-a00a2a53e56f" :question/math-academy-id "q-256564" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-4x{y}^{2}-x+6{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1,\\,0]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration. Round the answer for ${x}_{1}$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n| $[$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-2}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-3}} | $-8$ | $-2$ |  |  |  |\n\n| ${x}_{1}=[$ | $1$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-4x{y}^{2}-x+6{y}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}y-4x{y}^{2}-x+6{y}^{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4xy-4{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8xy+18{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-4{y}^{2}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-8xy+18{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-4{y}^{2}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-8xy+18{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4y & 4x-8y \\\\ 4x-8y & 36y-8x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}4y & 4x-8y \\\\ 4x-8y & 36y-8x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}4xy-4{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8xy+18{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}4y & 4x-8y \\\\ 4x-8y & 36y-8x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}4xy-4{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8xy+18{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4(0) & 4(1)-8(0) \\\\ 4(1)-8(0) & 36(0)-8(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}4(1)(0)-4(0)^{2}-1 \\\\ 2(1)^{2}-8(1)(0)+18(0)^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1/4\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15de0f5b-b26e-52ba-8242-dba89f34085e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15de0f5b-b26e-52ba-8242-dba89f34085e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380982} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8043409-855c-5c6d-a5d0-bf27f44cb3a7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202993} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "328336bf-d0b0-57a4-ab76-f25d3661b482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "328336bf-d0b0-57a4-ab76-f25d3661b482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380981} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc0a149-8377-5d67-94e4-f2670f3cc2dd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202994} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f85c28e4-0d36-56c1-9287-46852ba386d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380979} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcc1478-3a13-581a-8757-6d0d7d86861f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f85c28e4-0d36-56c1-9287-46852ba386d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380979} :answer-field/id #uuid "f88f5792-3730-5e6e-a6ce-69fd98a48005" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc5aadd2-f405-553a-a7ce-055d57e6d683" :question/math-academy-id "q-256567" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=5{x}^{2}y-4x+2{y}^{3}-6{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $10$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | $-6$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $10$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}y-4x+2{y}^{3}-6{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}y-4x+2{y}^{3}-6{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10xy-4 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-12y\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-12y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(10xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-12y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10y & 10x \\\\ 10x & 12y-12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}10y & 10x \\\\ 10x & 12y-12\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}10xy-4 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-12y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}10y & 10x \\\\ 10x & 12y-12\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}10xy-4 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-12y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10(1) & 10(1) \\\\ 10(1) & 12(1)-12\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}10(1)(1)-4 \\\\ 5(1)^{2}+6(1)^{2}-12(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 0\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50c72475-85fc-52ee-b4c7-80ae4f6092b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380985} {:answer/id #uuid "daa7556e-343d-53f4-9901-35b878f3e8c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50c72475-85fc-52ee-b4c7-80ae4f6092b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380985} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6d73a87-cba0-53d8-8575-d59070c26e9e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64fa56d7-5c20-5cd9-92c8-7562cd8d560f" :question/math-academy-id "q-257189" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,3]\\times [0,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=5x{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}-3xy-2x.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[2,\\,1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}=[$ | $0$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}-3xy-2x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}-3xy-2x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2 \\\\ 10xy-3x\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(10xy-3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(10xy-3x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 10y-3 \\\\ 10y-3 & 10x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 10y-3 \\\\ 10y-3 & 10x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2 \\\\ 10xy-3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 10y-3 \\\\ 10y-3 & 10x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2 \\\\ 10xy-3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 10(1)-3 \\\\ 10(1)-3 & 10(2)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-10(2)+5(1)^{2}-3(1)-2 \\\\ 10(2)(1)-3(2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-10)\\cdot 20-7\\cdot 7}[\\begin{aligned}20 & -7 \\\\ -7 & -10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{249}[\\begin{aligned}-498 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81b33150-b2ec-5ee5-9350-5940970994fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.406" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81b33150-b2ec-5ee5-9350-5940970994fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.406" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380989} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fc47041-c54b-5c7a-8e27-09d460849126" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c14fc02-43f0-5353-b2b2-d653536058c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-257190" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}+2xy-3x.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | $0.375$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}+2xy-3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}+2xy-3x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6xy-2x+2y-3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy-2x+2y-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+2x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy-2x+2y-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+2x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6y-2 & 6x+2 \\\\ 6x+2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}6y-2 & 6x+2 \\\\ 6x+2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}6xy-2x+2y-3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}6y-2 & 6x+2 \\\\ 6x+2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}6xy-2x+2y-3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6(2)-2 & 6(1)+2 \\\\ 6(1)+2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}6(1)(2)-2(1)+2(2)-3 \\\\ 3(1)^{2}+2(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}10 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{10\\cdot 0-8\\cdot 8}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{64}[\\begin{aligned}-40 \\\\ -38\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.375 \\\\ 1.406\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3715eb92-5e7c-5d33-aeca-0b8eddd08e6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380992} {:answer/id #uuid "171729a0-cb02-5ba5-bc43-7ad3acfb1520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.083" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "171729a0-cb02-5ba5-bc43-7ad3acfb1520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.083" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380993} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef2023fc-37ac-54be-a64f-8a630e9eb902" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6da593f1-899a-522b-9fef-fcbfae059a62" :question/math-academy-id "q-257193" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}-x+3{y}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[2,\\,0]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | $1.014$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}-x+3{y}^{2}+3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}-x+3{y}^{2}+3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6xy-2{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4xy+6y\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy-2{y}^{2}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-4xy+6y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy-2{y}^{2}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-4xy+6y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6y & 6x-4y \\\\ 6x-4y & 6-4x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}6y & 6x-4y \\\\ 6x-4y & 6-4x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}6xy-2{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4xy+6y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}6y & 6x-4y \\\\ 6x-4y & 6-4x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}6xy-2{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4xy+6y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6(0) & 6(2)-4(0) \\\\ 6(2)-4(0) & 6-4(2)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}6(2)(0)-2(0)^{2}-1 \\\\ 3(2)^{2}-4(2)(0)+6(0)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-2)-12\\cdot 12}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{144}[\\begin{aligned}-142 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.014 \\\\ 0.083\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bf088a9-161a-5738-bbf0-4e6583ebe130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.286" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bf088a9-161a-5738-bbf0-4e6583ebe130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.286" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380996} :answer-field/id #uuid "05e5f67a-6745-5171-9d79-1b9fac16de26" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380995} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dd80baa-d783-5122-8ed9-f459e9e7df42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.071" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dd80baa-d783-5122-8ed9-f459e9e7df42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.071" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380998} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c79023b-dd6d-526a-bae9-571b68deddf5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3f28da3-9a7b-5c08-a2a1-0df9f926279a" :question/math-academy-id "q-257197" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}-2xy+4x.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}-2xy+4x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}-2xy+4x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{2}-6xy-2x-2y+4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}y-3{x}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-6xy-2x-2y+4) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-3{x}^{2}-2x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-6xy-2x-2y+4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}y-3{x}^{2}-2x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-6y-2 & 4xy-6x-2 \\\\ 4xy-6x-2 & 2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-6y-2 & 4xy-6x-2 \\\\ 4xy-6x-2 & 2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{2}-6xy-2x-2y+4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}y-3{x}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-6y-2 & 4xy-6x-2 \\\\ 4xy-6x-2 & 2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{2}-6xy-2x-2y+4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}y-3{x}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(1)^{2}-6(1)-2 & 4(1)(1)-6(1)-2 \\\\ 4(1)(1)-6(1)-2 & 2(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2(1)(1)^{2}-6(1)(1)-2(1)-2(1)+4 \\\\ 2(1)^{2}(1)-3(1)^{2}-2(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-6)\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{28}[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.286 \\\\ 1.071\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4f65ae1-8356-5d36-9762-76558b0c33f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4f65ae1-8356-5d36-9762-76558b0c33f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381001} :answer-field/id #uuid "af0819e1-b366-566c-9514-78306690f436" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381000} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf7c1595-bcc4-500c-9a2f-ec837ce37131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf7c1595-bcc4-500c-9a2f-ec837ce37131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381003} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e4e5ff6-8e1f-57ca-87a2-8f05be911f5f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381002} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1099c091-c868-50a8-81f7-5744f4a77a0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1099c091-c868-50a8-81f7-5744f4a77a0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381005} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1343ad7-eccd-52b5-80f4-848b3a1f44e1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b06fbfd1-141e-56df-82a6-2eb49e9fa0b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-257203" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-8xy-y.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the pointt $[-1,\\,-1]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $-14$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | $-5$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-14$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-8xy-y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-8xy-y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}+6x-8y \\\\ 6xy-8x-1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}+6x-8y) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy-8x-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}+6x-8y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy-8x-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6y-8 \\\\ 6y-8 & 6x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6y-8 \\\\ 6y-8 & 6x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}+6x-8y \\\\ 6xy-8x-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6y-8 \\\\ 6y-8 & 6x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}+6x-8y \\\\ 6xy-8x-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & 6(-1)-8 \\\\ 6(-1)-8 & 6(-1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}3(-1)^{2}+6(-1)-8(-1) \\\\ 6(-1)(-1)-8(-1)-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -14 \\\\ -14 & -6\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac454fe6-8153-5f50-aa83-6df4ea850080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac454fe6-8153-5f50-aa83-6df4ea850080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381008} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc6c065b-4416-5d99-9659-7ae799aa3b94" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381007} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6e3bc29-d9da-54d5-af49-ab2ece5a783e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6e3bc29-d9da-54d5-af49-ab2ece5a783e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381010} :answer-field/id #uuid "0039370f-56f2-546e-a1e6-f7a67769f13c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381009} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab289e22-88a4-5dd6-abfb-b46ebbc6c2be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab289e22-88a4-5dd6-abfb-b46ebbc6c2be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381012} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ce90b6c-9019-5362-82d5-ffd81e83e2a6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "373fbe02-e9cb-5899-961d-b8f52b833d4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-257204" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=6{x}^{2}y+x+2y-2{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[-1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | $2$ | {{field-1}} | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | $1$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | $-4$ | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}y+x+2y-2{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}y+x+2y-2{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12xy+1-10x \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+2-4y\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(12xy+1-10x) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2-4y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(12xy+1-10x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2-4y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12y-10 & 12x \\\\ 12x & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}12y-10 & 12x \\\\ 12x & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}12xy+1-10x \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+2-4y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}12y-10 & 12x \\\\ 12x & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}12xy+1-10x \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+2-4y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}12(1)-10 & 12(-1) \\\\ 12(-1) & -4\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}12(-1)(1)+1-10(-1) \\\\ 6(-1)^{2}+2-4(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320c0243-6c23-5e9c-bb6b-26fed0fbd884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381015} {:answer/id #uuid "50839ad8-f33c-5fad-8998-3bf50a87fc73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "320c0243-6c23-5e9c-bb6b-26fed0fbd884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381015} :answer-field/id #uuid "d13409d1-5efe-5c30-8bc5-30e78a3554a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381014} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50d0fedf-e855-5099-9211-9e064d366991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50d0fedf-e855-5099-9211-9e064d366991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381018} :answer-field/id #uuid "26f3da8b-0dbe-5e3a-ba28-f87bdcbdcecb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381017} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f2beab8-4fa5-5bbe-b21c-c6cde82bac28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f2beab8-4fa5-5bbe-b21c-c6cde82bac28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381020} :answer-field/id #uuid "750904ff-b5e2-5bf3-a721-682a8439f217" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381019} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "582c2b1e-ed47-577c-a2c2-f64f631a7793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "582c2b1e-ed47-577c-a2c2-f64f631a7793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381022} :answer-field/id #uuid "89faeb07-bd9a-500e-b8f5-ab2e82d4acef" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "664db644-4112-587a-bd74-f12f11e11194" :question/math-academy-id "q-257205" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}-3x+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration. Round the answer for ${x}_{1}$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n| $[$ | $6$ | {{field-1}} | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-2}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-3}} | $-2$ | $-1$ |  |  |  |\n\n| ${x}_{1}=[$ | $0.75$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}-3x+{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}y-2x{y}^{2}-3x+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6xy-2{y}^{2}-3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4xy+2y\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy-2{y}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-4xy+2y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy-2{y}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-4xy+2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6y & 6x-4y \\\\ 6x-4y & -4x+2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}6y & 6x-4y \\\\ 6x-4y & -4x+2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}6xy-2{y}^{2}-3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4xy+2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}6y & 6x-4y \\\\ 6x-4y & -4x+2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}6xy-2{y}^{2}-3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4xy+2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6(1) & 6(1)-4(1) \\\\ 6(1)-4(1) & -4(1)+2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}6(1)(1)-2(1)^{2}-3 \\\\ 3(1)^{2}-4(1)(1)+2(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-1/4 \\\\ 1/4\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.75 \\\\ 1.25\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69011a96-9bbc-5267-bda8-49fceedd418b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.173" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381025} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a3ae50-ef89-5dd6-a147-417164758fc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69011a96-9bbc-5267-bda8-49fceedd418b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.173" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381025} :answer-field/id #uuid "e537451b-39d4-5ba9-9076-55a6f69274dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381024} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed8624d8-c571-53b8-b3c2-71d5055fc762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.251" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed8624d8-c571-53b8-b3c2-71d5055fc762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.251" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381028} :answer-field/id #uuid "b43a093d-e7a6-5cdf-9aab-37fd747d44f7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fa98aa3-1120-5cee-97d1-0d51a43712e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-257209" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [-1,0]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=4{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5y.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1.2,\\,-0.3]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8xy+2x \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2y-5\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy+2x) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}+2y-5) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(8xy+2x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}+2y-5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8y+2 & 8x \\\\ 8x & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}8y+2 & 8x \\\\ 8x & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}8xy+2x \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2y-5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}8y+2 & 8x \\\\ 8x & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}8xy+2x \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2y-5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.2 \\\\ -0.3\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}8(-0.3)+2 & 8(1.2) \\\\ 8(1.2) & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}8(1.2)(-0.3)+2(1.2) \\\\ 4(1.2)^{2}+2(-0.3)-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.2 \\\\ -0.3\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-0.4 & 9.6 \\\\ 9.6 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-0.48 \\\\ 0.16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.2 \\\\ -0.3\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-0.4)\\cdot 2-(9.6)\\cdot (9.6)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9.6 \\\\ -9.6 & -0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-0.48 \\\\ 0.16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.2 \\\\ -0.3\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{92.96}[\\begin{aligned}-2.496 \\\\ 4.544\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.173 \\\\ -0.251\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dae0ee30-487d-5c0d-8557-835b8387316b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dae0ee30-487d-5c0d-8557-835b8387316b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381031} :answer-field/id #uuid "44324cb0-9c59-535e-a205-1c6a563eff72" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381030} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e269dc3c-f5a9-5af3-8efb-4e5d7accb00a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.519" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e269dc3c-f5a9-5af3-8efb-4e5d7accb00a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.519" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381033} :answer-field/id #uuid "af5de88c-c650-5862-a836-17875c0132cb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00031552-df48-530a-af4f-8ca031bae430" :question/math-academy-id "q-257210" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=-4{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-3y.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[0.2,\\,0.3]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-3y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-3y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8xy+2x+2 \\\\ -4{x}^{2}+2y-3\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-8xy+2x+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4{x}^{2}+2y-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-8xy+2x+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4{x}^{2}+2y-3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8y+2 & -8x \\\\ -8x & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}-8y+2 & -8x \\\\ -8x & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}-8xy+2x+2 \\\\ -4{x}^{2}+2y-3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}-8y+2 & -8x \\\\ -8x & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}-8xy+2x+2 \\\\ -4{x}^{2}+2y-3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-8(0.3)+2 & -8(0.2) \\\\ -8(0.2) & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-8(0.2)(0.3)+2(0.2)+2 \\\\ -4(0.2)^{2}+2(0.3)-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-0.4 & -1.6 \\\\ -1.6 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1.92 \\\\ -2.56\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-0.4)\\cdot 2-(-1.6)\\cdot (-1.6)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1.6 \\\\ 1.6 & -0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1.92 \\\\ -2.56\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{3.36}[\\begin{aligned}-0.256 \\\\ 4.096\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.124 \\\\ 1.519\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e86886b8-e70f-58ac-a832-35115e4e2f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.148" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e86886b8-e70f-58ac-a832-35115e4e2f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.148" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381036} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5b82125-49e2-5b90-9e8d-8f1ae38fb386" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e438366-4ded-5d1e-a3c1-0958c57cad51" :question/math-academy-id "q-257219" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}-8.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-2,\\,-2]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-1.4,\\,0.4]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | $-0.07$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}-8) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}-8)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4x+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2xy+10y\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+10y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+10y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x+10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x+10\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}-4x+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2xy+10y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x+10\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}-4x+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2xy+10y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.4 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2(0.4) \\\\ 2(0.4) & 2(-1.4)+10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-4(-1.4)+(0.4)^{2} \\\\ 2(-1.4)(0.4)+10(0.4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.4 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.8 & 7.2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5.76 \\\\ 2.88\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.4 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot (7.2)-(0.8)\\cdot (0.8)}[\\begin{aligned}7.2 & -0.8 \\\\ -0.8 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5.76 \\\\ 2.88\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.4 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{29.44}[\\begin{aligned}39.168 \\\\ -16.128\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.07 \\\\ -0.148\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b38d3c6b-7fdc-53d8-81d9-9cf38c92a875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.442" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b38d3c6b-7fdc-53d8-81d9-9cf38c92a875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.442" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381039} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef4c68ed-fd56-5ba1-8fcf-9b50a652c9ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72c39b74-68e2-57de-b9f2-483f0bcb86a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-257220" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [-1,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}-xy-{y}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[2,\\,3]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[0.96,\\,-0.48]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-0.221$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-xy-{y}^{2}+3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-xy-{y}^{2}+3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-y \\\\ -x-2y\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-y) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x-2y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x-2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6x & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}6x & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-y \\\\ -x-2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}6x & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-y \\\\ -x-2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.96 \\\\ -0.48\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6(0.96) & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3(0.96)^{2}-(-0.48) \\\\ -0.96-2(-0.48)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.96 \\\\ -0.48\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}5.76 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3.2448 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.96 \\\\ -0.48\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{5.76\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5.76\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3.2448 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.96 \\\\ -0.48\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{12.52}[\\begin{aligned}-6.4896 \\\\ 3.2448\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.442 \\\\ -0.221\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6c21214-f358-58b6-8e57-92d94a5f9d35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381042} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3b96cb-77af-513d-8bd7-4bb760aa6642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6c21214-f358-58b6-8e57-92d94a5f9d35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381042} :answer-field/id #uuid "feb694c5-8f06-5b52-86c6-8ab7253d187b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381041} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aea42af-0700-57c3-a020-ffbc7173b6f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0aea42af-0700-57c3-a020-ffbc7173b6f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381045} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1489fa7-3144-53c6-b8b4-0b8ecad61c19" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3542bf2-b1a1-58de-8ed1-85bce925d666" :question/math-academy-id "q-257223" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,32]\\times [1,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+6.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1.5,\\,1.5]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[2.25,\\,2.25]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\n| ${x}_{2}=[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6y\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-6x) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-6y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-6x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-6y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6x-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y-6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}6x-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y-6\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}6x-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y-6\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.25 \\\\ 2.25\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6(2.25)-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6(2.25)-6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3(2.25)^{2}-6(2.25) \\\\ 3(2.25)^{2}-6(2.25)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.25 \\\\ 2.25\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}7.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7.5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1.6875 \\\\ 1.6875\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.25 \\\\ 2.25\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{7.5\\cdot 7.5-0\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}7.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7.5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1.6875 \\\\ 1.6875\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.25 \\\\ 2.25\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{56.25}[\\begin{aligned}12.656\\,25 \\\\ 12.656\\,25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.025 \\\\ 2.025\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
